<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Unbranding Coca-Cola</title>
    <style>
        /* Set overall page styles to mimic a simple corporate landing page, using red and white for Coca-Cola branding */
        body {
            background-color: #f40000; /* Coca-Cola's signature red */
            color: white;
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif; /* Simple, web-safe font */
            text-align: center;
            margin: 0;
            padding: 50px 20px;
        }

        /* Header styling for the main title */
        h1 {
            font-size: 48px;
            margin-bottom: 20px;
        }

        /* Paragraph styling for body text */
        p {
            font-size: 24px;
            max-width: 800px;
            margin: 0 auto 40px;
        }

        /* Logo image styling: centered, sized appropriately, with a subtle shadow for depth */
        #logo {
            width: 400px; /* Scalable size for visibility */
            height: auto;
            display: block;
            margin: 0 auto;
            box-shadow: 0 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3); /* Adds a subtle 3D effect */
            transition: transform 0.3s ease; /* Smooth hover transition for interactivity feel */
        }

        /* Additional hover effect on the logo container for visual feedback */
        #logo:hover {
            transform: scale(1.05); /* Slight zoom on hover to enhance engagement */
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Coca-Cola: The Real Thing</h1>
    <img id="logo" src="original.png" alt="Original Poison-Cola Logo">
    <p>Refresh yourself with the world's favorite beverage. Happiness in every sip.</p>

    <!-- Statement explaining the meaning and motivation: This unbranding project parodies Coca-Cola to critique its role in promoting high-sugar drinks that contribute to global health crises like obesity, diabetes, and tooth decay. The original logo is swapped on hover with an unbranded version (e.g., modified to read "Coca-Coma" with imagery suggesting health risks) to juxtapose the glossy corporate image with the harsh reality of its impact on public health and corporate profiteering from addiction. The motivation is to challenge how corporations use design and marketing to mask harmful practices and maintain hegemony over consumer habits. I chose Coca-Cola as it's an iconic brand symbolizing consumerism. -->

    <script>
        // Get the logo element by its ID
        const logo = document.getElementById('logo');

        // Add mouseover event to swap to the unbranded image
        logo.addEventListener('mouseover', () => {
            logo.src = 'unbranded.png'; // Changes the image source to the parody version for critique reveal
        });

        // Add mouseout event to revert to the original image
        logo.addEventListener('mouseout', () => {
            logo.src = 'original.png'; // Restores the original corporate image
        });

        // Explanation for JavaScript code: This code uses event listeners to handle mouseover and mouseout events on the image element, swapping the 'src' attribute between the original and unbranded images. I'm using it to create the required interactive rollover effect that juxtaposes the corporate identity with the critique. This is standard DOM manipulation in JavaScript, based on examples from MDN Web Docs (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Element/mouseover_event and https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Element/mouseout_event). It's not from the book or class examples, so I'm documenting it here.
    </script>
</body>
</html>
